WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:12.000
Since we talked about text fields, it's very important to talk about forms, the place text fields are used most. We use them in many situations across all kinds of products: when we

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:21.500
buy, when we log in, when we enter our details, and in many other cases. So let's look at some guidelines for forms in general, and for text fields inside forms in particular.

3
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:30.500
The first guideline is that we always need to keep the form as short as possible. How do we keep it short? First we review what

4
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:39.500
we put in the form: once we've written down all the data we need to collect from the user, we go back over it: is there data

5
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:48.500
we can get later in some way? Is there data that isn't necessary to ask for? Then we start removing that data as much as we can.

6
00:00:48.500 --> 00:01:00.167
Second: we try to keep the things in the form that belong together visually close, or clearly one group. In this example, look at the word Password: the Password label, which belongs to the

7
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:11.833
second field, sits at almost the same distance from the first field as from the second. So it isn't clear that Password is closer to the second field and belongs to it, even though it

8
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:23.500
obviously does. But when the form is long, this gets worse, and it may look like supporting text for the first field. So I always try to bring the label close to its own field.

9
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:34.500
Also, if I'm collecting different kinds of data, I can split it into sections: if I ask for personal information, say, and then payment information, I split them into Personal and Payment, and so on. That makes it easier for the

10
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:45.500
user to understand what each section is about and what exactly the data is for. It also makes it easier to finish one section and move on to the next, rather than one long form, which can be annoying at times.

11
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:55.167
Third: we always try to stack the fields or any inputs in a form under each other, rather than in two columns side by side, because that breaks the eye's path as I

12
00:01:55.167 --> 00:02:04.833
go down vertically finishing things one under another. Unless, of course, there's a logic for putting things side by side that makes it better. Like the expiration date and the CVV: they belong together

13
00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:14.500
in the payment method, and the CVV is a short number anyway, so we can put them in two columns side by side. Otherwise it's always better to keep them in a single column.

14
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:24.636
The next point is using a logical sequence. How do I decide whether to use one? I can ask: while the user enters this data, will they be doing something specific, or

15
00:02:24.636 --> 00:02:34.772
taking the data from somewhere? Like this example: if they're taking the data from a credit card, they're looking at the front of the card, reading the card number, then the expiration

16
00:02:34.772 --> 00:02:44.908
date, then turning the card over to read the CVV on the back. That's the order they follow, and that's the logic I use to lay out the fields: card number first, then

17
00:02:44.908 --> 00:02:55.044
the expiration date, then the CVV, so they turn it over and read it from the back. Also, if things have a very clear logical link, like first name and last name,

18
00:02:55.044 --> 00:03:05.180
of course first name comes first, then last name. So I start asking: is there anything that should make me put the fields in a certain order, or will the order not matter?
